<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; padding-bottom: 5px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # These Plants Are from Panama. – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-style: italic; margin: 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Helping in the Garden</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> These Plants Are from Panama.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Participate in discussions about school events.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow simple stories and descriptions about planting and watering (e.g., 'I water the plants every day.').
            Can participate in discussions about school garden tasks, answering simple questions based on oral information (e.g., 'What do they grow in the garden?' 'They grow tomatoes.' 'How often do they water the tomatoes?' 'Every day.').
            Can get the gist of a conversation on caring for plants (e.g., 'What time do they water the plants?' 'In the morning.').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f0f8ff;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing flashcards of garden items (e.g., 'garden,' 'plant,' 'tomato,' 'watering can,' 'soil,' 'seed') and asks, "What do you see?" Students listen and respond chorally or individually with the names of the items. The teacher plays a short audio clip of garden sounds (birds, water sprinkling, rustling leaves) and asks, "What sounds do you hear? Where are we?" to introduce the 'Helping in the Garden' scenario.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard of a 'tomato' and explicitly models the pronunciation of /t/ in 'tomatoes'. Then, the teacher holds up a flashcard of a 'plant' and models 'grow,' emphasizing the /g/ sound. The teacher then says simple sentences using target vocabulary and grammar, like "I water the plants every day," and "This is a tomato plant," pointing to visuals. Students listen and repeat chorally. The teacher also models describing simple gardening steps: "First, plant the seeds. Second, water the plant."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding a 'tomato' flashcard) "Is this a 'potato' or a 'tomato'?" (To check /t/ sound and vocabulary)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding a 'watering can' flashcard) "Do we use this to 'water' the plants or 'eat' the plants?" (To check understanding of 'water' verb)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to soil) "Is this 'soil' for the plants or 'water' for the plants?" (To check understanding of 'soil')</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Helping in the Garden".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio story / dialogue between two children, Maria and Pedro, discussing their tasks in the school garden. The dialogue is simple and repetitive, using the target vocabulary and grammar structures. (Genre: narrative excerpt, Register: informal, Format: audio recording).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The learner listens to short, simple descriptions and discussions about routine activities related to helping in the garden, identifying key information and answering basic questions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Audio Transcript Example:</b><br/>
                Maria: "Look, Pedro! These are our tomato plants. They grow so fast!"<br/>
                Pedro: "Yes! Do you like watering the plants, Maria?"<br/>
                Maria: "I love watering the plants! I water the plants every day, in the morning. And you, Pedro? Do you like digging?"<br/>
                Pedro: "I don't like digging, but I like planting the seeds in the soil. First, we put the seed, and then we add water."<br/>
                Maria: "These tomatoes are small, but they are very tasty! We need sunlight for them to grow healthy."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Listening):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> (Teacher plays the audio once) "Who is talking in the story?" (Maria and Pedro) "Where are they?" (In the garden) "What are they talking about?" (Helping with plants).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> (Teacher plays the audio a second time) Students listen and mark True or False for sentences like: "Maria waters the plants every day." (True) "Pedro likes digging." (False) "They grow flowers in the garden." (False - tomatoes) "These are small tomatoes." (True).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> The teacher projects images (Maria, Pedro, watering can, seed, tomato). Students listen for details and match names to actions. E.g., "Maria <b>waters</b> the plants." (Students point to Maria and watering can). "Pedro <b>plants</b> the seeds." (Students point to Pedro and seeds).</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening to identify present simple sentences with 's' for third person singular, demonstratives ('These are,' 'This is'), question forms ('How often do you?', 'Do you like?'), and garden vocabulary (garden, plant, tomato, seed, soil, watering can, grow, water, every day, in the morning).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled practice (Listen and Circle):</b> The teacher reads sentences aloud. Students have a worksheet with sentences containing two options. They listen and circle the correct word or phrase. Example: "Maria (water / <b>waters</b>) the plants every day." "Pedro (likes / <b>like</b>) planting seeds." "These (is / <b>are</b>) tomato plants." Focus on Present Simple + 's' and demonstratives.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled (Listen and Fill-in-the-Blanks):</b> The teacher plays a slightly modified version of the dialogue or new short sentences with strategic gaps. Students have a word bank (e.g., 'soil,' 'seed,' 'grow,' 'water,' 'garden'). They listen and write the missing word. Example: "First, plant the _____. Then, _____ the plants." "The plants need _____ to _____." (soil, seed, water, sunlight, grow). This activity reinforces vocabulary and sentence structure while listening.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction / Peer Check (Listen and Confirm):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher reads short questions about the garden scenario (e.g., "What do they grow in the garden?" "How often does Maria water the plants?" "What time do they water the plants?"). After each question, students listen, think, and then whisper the answer to their partner. They check if their partner's answer matches theirs, based on what they heard. The teacher circulates, listening to student responses and providing feedback.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">13 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Helping in the Garden."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Listen and Sequence" - Students will listen to a short audio recording describing gardening steps and then arrange sequential picture cards in the correct order. This is a listening comprehension task focused on understanding instructions and sequence words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher distributes sets of four picture cards to small groups (3-4 students). The cards depict simple gardening actions: 1) a hand digging a hole, 2) a hand planting a seed, 3) a hand covering the seed with soil, 4) a watering can watering the plant.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher explains: "Listen carefully to the instructions. Put the pictures in the correct order."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays an audio recording of simple instructions: "Hello, children! Let's plant a seed! <b>First</b>, dig a hole in the soil. <b>Second</b>, put the seed in the hole. <b>Third</b>, cover the seed with soil. <b>Fourth</b>, water the plant. Do this every day, and your plant will grow!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen to the audio and collaboratively arrange their picture cards.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After the first listen, the teacher plays the audio again for students to check their sequence.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher checks the groups' sequences, asking, "What is first? What is second?" to reinforce listening comprehension and sequence words.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Listen and Sequence" activity and a simple "Listen and Match" worksheet.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students complete a "Listen and Match" activity individually. The teacher provides a worksheet with three short descriptions read aloud (e.g., "This is a red vegetable. It grows in the garden.") and three corresponding pictures (e.g., a tomato, a watering can, a flower). Students listen to each description and draw a line to match it to the correct picture. This assesses comprehension of specific vocabulary and simple descriptive sentences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Listening):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to match descriptions to correct pictures (accuracy of listening comprehension).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to follow simple oral instructions (for "Listen and Sequence" task).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Gist comprehension of garden-related conversations.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete one of the following on a small slip of paper:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I heard today is: _______."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do you do in the garden? (Circle one) water / dig / eat" (They circle what they heard as a task).</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to understand when listening today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part was difficult to understand?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in this listening lesson? Students seemed engaged with the audio stories and flashcards, and the "Listen and Sequence" task was effective for confirming comprehension of instructions. The pronunciation practice for /t/ and /g/ needs more explicit modeling and repetition. Adjustments for the next lesson should include more varied short audio clips and possibly differentiating the "Listen and Match" task with different complexity levels for faster and slower listeners. I need to ensure more individual listening checks throughout. More use of "like" and "don't like" sentences should be integrated to reinforce preferences while listening.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson introduced core gardening vocabulary and simple routines through listening. The next lesson (Reading Comprehension) will use these same vocabulary words and basic grammar structures in a short written text about the school garden, allowing students to reinforce understanding through visual word recognition and contextual reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>